WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.057 --> 00:00:03.640
SPEAKER: Welcome to Google Cloud
Drawing Board where we doodle

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.900
our way through the cloud.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.500
Today's topic, what
is Cloud Storage?

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.590
Cloud Storage is a global
secure and scalable object store

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.360
for immutable data such
as images, text, videos,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.090
or any other file formats.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.160
You store objects
in buckets, which

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.960
are associated with
a project, which are

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.120
grouped under an organization.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.240
You can then upload objects to
the bucket and download objects

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.290
from it using the console
or gsutil commands.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.430
By default, data at
rest is encrypted.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.680
You have options to secure
it with your own encryption

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.560
keys using Google Cloud's
Key Management service

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.420
or at your own key management
service on premise.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.110
You can grant permission to
specific members and teams

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.930
or make the objects fully public
for use cases such as websites.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:46.870
When creating buckets,
you have options

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.390
depending on budget,
availability, and access

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.500
frequency.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.630
Choose standard regional
or multiregional buckets

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.520
for high performance,
frequent access,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.370
and highest availability,
Nearline for once a month

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.250
access, Coldline for
less than once a quarter,

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.190
and Archive for data that you
want to put away for years.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.410
It costs a bit more to
use standard storage

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.680
because it allows for automatic
redundancy and frequent access

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.600
options.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.650
Nearline, Coldline,
and Archive storage

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.180
offer 99% availability and
cost significantly less.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.250
Cloud Storage offers
automatic object

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.500
versioning so you don't have
to worry about versioning

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.270
and can still access the
older version if needed.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.840
With Object
Lifecycle Management,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.970
you can automatically transition
the data to lower cost storage

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:29.680
classes when it
reaches a certain age,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.480
or when you store a
newer version of a file.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.420
Once you have stored
the data, accessing it

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.290
is easy with a single API
call for all storage classes.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.270
Standard regional and
multiregional buckets

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.580
are optimal to use for
hosting static websites,

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.560
streaming, and
storing documents.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.270
Nearline and Coldline
storage classes

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.240
are widely used for backups
and disaster recovery,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.530
and Archive, as
the name suggests,

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.980
is best for long term archiving.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.320
Cloud Storage is a
great data lake choice

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.150
for streaming and
batch uploading data

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.827
for big data and ML projects.

00:01:59.827 --> 00:02:01.660
To [? new ?] [? to more ?]
on Cloud Storage,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.330
check out
cloud.google.com/storage

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:05.580
